Question 6/20 – Worker Node Failure in EKS
Advanced troubleshooting
Scenario:

Your EKS cluster has:
- 2 worker nodes (t3.small)
- 3 application Pods
- HPA minimum replicas: 2
- HPA maximum replicas: 6
- CPU target: 50%
Suddenly, one worker node crashes.

The remaining node is running two application Pods.

Answer these questions:
1. What happens to the Pods that were running on the failed worker node?
A: out of the 2 worker nodes, if 1 node failes then k8s will detect it through node heart beats and marks the node not ready and the pods which were on the node1 
   will be un-available and then the replacement pods are created in node 2 if the sufficient resources are available 
   Deployments maintain the desired number of replicas through ReplicaSets.

2. Will Kubernetes automatically create replacement Pods? Explain how.
A: yes k8s will create new replacement pods to match the desired state of the replica set. how means: if k8s detects a node failure, then it marks the pods 
   on the node unavailable, and now replica set notices that there are few pods, so it will create new pods and schefuler places them on healthy nodes
   
3. Will HPA automatically increase the number of replicas because a worker node crashed?
A: no as hpa is for scaling pods. not for worker nodes. this is done by deployment, replica set and asg 

4. How would you ensure your application remains available even if one worker node fails?
A: to ensure availability: we will try to have worker nodes in diff availability zones, spare cpu and memory on healthy nodes, pod distrubution in worker nodes, 
                           asg implementation

5. Which Kubernetes feature would you use to distribute Pods across different worker nodes?
A: Pod anti affinity
   suppose we have 3 pods, without distribution rules k8s can schedule all pods on one node. to avoid this we use pod-anti affinity to distribute pods accross nodes.
   in the deployment file we configure this pod affinity rules.

Bonus: Explain the difference between Pod anti-affinity and topology spread constraints.
A: Pod affinity: avoids placing matching pods together, uses affinity rules, Don't put two matching Pods on the same node.
   Topology spread: distributes pods are evenly as possible or in different availability zones


